From b759b6431967449a61c6ac6918192f39c01f6cfe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:46:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] CDVDDemuxFFmpeg: a VC-1 stream of a Blu-ray opens at the rate
 its playlist declares, not twice it

FFmpeg sets r_frame_rate of a codec that can code fields (VC-1 among them)
to twice the rate its sequence header declares, and av_guess_frame_rate(),
which Kodi takes, gives the declared rate back only once the average rate
is known: avformat_find_stream_info() knows it after the third picture of
the stream, and stops as soon as every stream of the clip has its
parameters, which on a Blu-ray (its audio, its PG streams) can be before
that. The stream then opened at 47.952 instead of 23.976 (50 for 25, 59.94
for 29.97), and the rate measured during playback switched the display a
few seconds into each clip. Measured with Hades' FFmpeg 9.0.2 on synthetic
VC-1 clips (with and without pulldown, which plays no part in it).

Now, for a VC-1 stream of a Blu-ray whose rate from FFmpeg is exactly
twice the one its sequence header declares, with no average rate, and
whose play item's stream number table declares that same rate, the
declared rate is taken: the one FFmpeg gives once its analysis reads the
third picture (BlurayVc1Rate.h). Any other stream, and any other input, is
as before. Said in the log when it is taken.

The idea is SamuriHL's (CoreELEC, "take a VC-1 stream's frame rate from the
playlist when ffmpeg doubles it"); the cause, the condition and the code
are Hades' own.
